<section xml:id="section-double-integrals-rectangles" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Double and Iterated Integrals Over Rectangles</title>

<p>
Before we begin discussing the actual topic of today's lecture, we will
briefly remind ourselves of the concept of area under a function and its
relation with definite integrals.
</p>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-area-under-curve-reminder">
<title>Reminder: Area Under <m>y = f(x)</m> from <m>x = a</m> to <m>x = b</m></title>

<p>
As shown in <xref ref="fig-riemann-1d-prefigure"/>, to calculate the
area under <m>y = f(x)</m> from <m>x = a</m> to <m>x = b</m>, we divide
the interval <m>(a,b)</m> into <m>n</m> subintervals and then let the
number of subintervals (which is the same as the number of rectangles)
approach infinity. Write the area as such a limit, and then as the
definite integral it defines.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,5.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the area as a limit of a sum of rectangle areas and as a definite integral.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-riemann-1d-prefigure">
<caption>
The area under <m>y = f(x)</m> from <m>x = a</m> to <m>x = b</m>,
approximated by <m>n = 8</m> rectangles of width <m>\Delta x</m>. The
height of the <m>i</m>-th rectangle is <m>f(x_i^*)</m>, where
<m>x_i^*</m> is a sample point in the <m>i</m>-th subinterval. As
<m>n \to \infty</m>, the total area of the rectangles approaches the
area under the curve.
</caption>
<image width="80%">
<shortdescription>
The area under a curve approximated by eight rectangles.
</shortdescription>
