<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-double-integrals-rectangles">
<title>Double Integrals Over Rectangles</title>

<p>
As we will see, double integrals can be interpreted geometrically as
the volume under a surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m>. As an example, consider
the surface <m>z = x^2 + y + 5</m> over the region
</p>

<me>
R : \{(x,y) : 0 \le x \le 2,\ 0 \le y \le 2\},
</me>

<p>
drawn in <xref ref="fig-volume-solid-geogebra"/>. The solid we are after
stands on top of <m>R</m> and stops at the surface: its floor is the
shaded square <m>R</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane, its ceiling is the piece
of the surface lying directly above <m>R</m>, outlined in dashed black,
and the dashed vertical segments above the four corners of <m>R</m> are
its vertical edges. Computing the volume of that solid<mdash/>the volume
on top of the region of integration and below the surface<mdash/>is the
goal of this section. Its ceiling is curved, so no formula from solid
geometry gives the answer, and we fall back on the idea of
<xref ref="sec-area-under-curve-reminder"/>: estimate the volume with
pieces whose volume we do know, then refine.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-volume-solid-geogebra">
<caption>
The volume this section sets out to compute: the solid above the region
of integration <m>R = [0,2]\times[0,2]</m> and below the surface
<m>z = x^2 + y + 5</m>. The shaded square is <m>R</m>, the dashed black
curve is the outline of the top of the solid on the surface, and the
dashed vertical segments are the edges of the solid above the four
corners of <m>R</m>.
</caption>
<!-- the surface, the colours and the camera of the applet of
     fig-double-integral-geogebra, with the boxes removed and the top
     outline, the corner drops and the region of integration added -->
<interactive platform="geogebra" width="100%" aspect="600:450">
<slate surface="geogebra" source="geogebra/volume-solid.ggb"
       aspect="600:450" material-width="600" material-height="450"/>
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the picture and scroll to zoom. Rotating is
worth doing here: from the opening angle the far half of the dashed
outline lies behind the surface.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<p>
To estimate that volume, use the point <m>(1,1)</m> in the middle of
<m>R</m> to find the height of a single cuboid standing on <m>R</m>, and
compute this (very bad) estimate of the desired volume.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,3.25) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the one-box estimate of the volume.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-volume-one-box-geogebra">
<caption>
A first, very rough estimate of the volume under
<m>z = x^2 + y + 5</m> over the square <m>R = [0,2]\times[0,2]</m>: a
single cuboid whose height is sampled at the center of the square. Turn
the picture around: seen from any direction, part of the box sticks out
above the surface and part of the volume under the surface is left
uncovered.
</caption>
<!-- the construction, and the colours, of the applet of
     fig-double-integral-geogebra, held at one division in each
     direction; sized to the text column so it is not scaled -->
<interactive platform="geogebra" width="100%" aspect="600:450">
<slate surface="geogebra" source="geogebra/one-box-estimate.ggb"
       aspect="600:450" material-width="600" material-height="450"/>
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the picture and scroll to zoom. The base of
the box is all of <m>R</m>, and its height is the value of <m>f</m> at the
center of <m>R</m>.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<p>
We can improve the estimate by splitting the region into more squares.
Split <m>R</m> into four squares, sample <m>f</m> at the center of each,
and add up the four cuboids, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-volume-four-boxes-geogebra"/>. Similar to finding the area
under <m>f(x)</m>, if we let the number of splittings go to infinity,
our estimation becomes exact, i.e., we find the exact desired volume.
The refinement process is animated in <xref ref="fig-riemann-boxes-video"/>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,5.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the four-box estimate of the volume.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-volume-four-boxes-geogebra">
<caption>
Splitting <m>R</m> into four squares improves the estimate. Each cuboid
uses the value of <m>f</m> at the center of its square. Turn the
picture around: each box now has to match the surface over a quarter of
<m>R</m> instead of over all of it, as the single box of
<xref ref="fig-volume-one-box-geogebra"/> does.
</caption>
<!-- as fig-volume-one-box-geogebra: the construction and the colours of
     the applet of fig-double-integral-geogebra, held at two divisions in
     each direction -->
<interactive platform="geogebra" width="100%" aspect="600:450">
<slate surface="geogebra" source="geogebra/four-boxes-estimate.ggb"
       aspect="600:450" material-width="600" material-height="450"/>
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the picture and scroll to zoom. Each box
stands on one of the four squares, and its height is the value of
<m>f</m> at the center of that square.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-double-integral-geogebra">
<caption>
A Riemann sum for a double integral, to explore: the region of integration,
the surface <m>z=f(x,y)</m>, and the grid of boxes built over it. Refining
the grid drives the sum toward <m>\iint_R f(x,y)\,dA</m>.
</caption>
<!-- A 600 by 450 copy of the published material nfwgbjnu, with its 3D
     view aimed exactly as in fig-volume-one-box-geogebra and
     fig-volume-four-boxes-geogebra: same centre (1,1,5) and the same
     three scales.  A published material cannot be re-aimed from the
     page, so the file is served locally; the label of f is moved to the
     lower right because the slider panel narrows the 3D pane. -->
<interactive platform="geogebra" width="100%" aspect="600:450">
<slate surface="geogebra" source="geogebra/riemann-sum-sliders.ggb"
       aspect="600:450" material-width="600" material-height="450"/>
<instructions>
<p>
Use the two sliders to set the number of divisions in the <m>x</m><ndash/>
and <m>y</m>-directions. Each box of the grid contributes its base area times
the height of the surface above it, and the sum of those volumes is the
estimate of the double integral. Increase the number of divisions and watch
the estimate settle down. Drag the background to rotate the picture and
scroll to zoom.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<p>
Write the approximation of the volume in Riemann-sum notation, using
<m>n</m> divisions in <m>x</m> and <m>m</m> in <m>y</m>, and then the
exact volume as a limit. (Each box contributes its base area
<m>\Delta A_{ij} = \Delta x_i\,\Delta y_j</m> times the height of the
surface at a sample point.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,5.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the Riemann sum approximating the volume and for its limit.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="assemblage-double-integral-def">
<title>The Double Integral Over a Rectangle</title>
<p>
The result of the above limit is defined as <term>the double integral
over a rectangular region</term> and is denoted as
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-double-integral-def">
\iint_R f(x,y)\,dA
\quad\text{or}\quad
\iint_R f(x,y)\,dx\,dy.
</men>
</assemblage>

<p>
For instance, in the example above, write the volume as a double
integral, with its limits.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,3.25) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the volume of the example written as a double integral with limits.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
Next, we will study how to calculate such integrals.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-riemann-boxes-video">
<caption>
Animation of the Riemann-sum definition of the double integral. The
region <m>R = [0,2]\times[0,2]</m> is split into <m>1</m>, then
<m>4</m>, then <m>16</m>, then <m>64</m> squares, and a cuboid is
built over each square with height sampled at its center. The
displayed estimate approaches the exact volume, which you will compute
in <xref ref="example-iterated-rectangle"/>.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-riemann-boxes"
       source="videos/riemann_boxes_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-iterated-integrals">
<title>Calculating Double Integrals as Iterated Integrals</title>

<example xml:id="example-iterated-rectangle">
<title>Example I</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the double integral
<m>\displaystyle\int_0^2\!\!\int_0^2 \left(x^2 + y + 5\right)\,dx\,dy</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,7.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for computing the double integral of Example I.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<p>
In calculating double integrals, the following theorem can be very
useful.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-fubini">
<title>Fubini's Theorem (First Form)</title>
<statement>
<p>
If <m>f(x,y)</m> is continuous throughout the rectangular region
<m>R: a \le x \le b,\ c \le y \le d</m>, then
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-fubini-rectangle">
\iint_R f(x,y)\,dA
= \int_c^d\!\!\int_a^b f(x,y)\,dx\,dy
= \int_a^b\!\!\int_c^d f(x,y)\,dy\,dx.
</men>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
Therefore, if the integrand satisfies the conditions of
<xref ref="thm-fubini"/>, we can change the order of integration as in
<xref ref="eq-fubini-rectangle"/>. Check this on the previous example:
integrate over <m>y</m> first, then over <m>x</m>, and compare with the
result of <xref ref="example-iterated-rectangle"/>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,7.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for computing the same integral in the other order.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-iterated-integral-substitution">
<title>An Iterated Integral Requiring Substitution and Integration by Parts</title>

<example xml:id="example-iterated-substitution">
<title>Example II</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the double integral
<m>\displaystyle\int_0^2\!\!\int_0^1 \frac{x}{1+xy}\,dx\,dy</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,15);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,14.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for computing the double integral of Example II.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-substitution-surface-tikz">
<caption>
The surface <m>z = \dfrac{x}{1+xy}</m> over the rectangle
<m>0 \le x \le 1</m>, <m>0 \le y \le 2</m>. The double integral of
<xref ref="example-iterated-substitution"/> is the volume between this
surface and the shaded base rectangle.
</caption>
<image width="75%">
<shortdescription>
A curved surface over a rectangle in the plane, highest along one edge.
</shortdescription>
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space with a curved surface over the
rectangle zero to one in x and zero to two in y. The surface rises
from height zero along the y z plane to height one at the corner x
equals one, y equals zero, and slopes downward as y increases. The
base rectangle in the x y plane is shaded.
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={105}{22},
      width=12.5cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=0, xmax=1.35, ymin=0, ymax=2.6, zmin=0, zmax=1.35,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % ---- shaded base rectangle ----
    \fill[oivermillion, opacity=0.18]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:1,0,0)
      -- (axis cs:1,2,0) -- (axis cs:0,2,0) -- cycle;
    \draw[oivermillion, line width=0.9pt]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:1,0,0) -- (axis cs:1,2,0)
      -- (axis cs:0,2,0) -- cycle;
    % ---- surface z = x/(1+xy) ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=faceted, faceted color=black!40,
              point meta={z},
              colormap={softz}{color=(oiblue!10!white) color=(oiblue!38!white)},
              opacity=0.55, fill opacity=0.55,
              domain=0:1, samples=16,
              y domain=0:2, samples y=16,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      (x, y, {x/(1+x*y)});
    % ---- drops from the surface at (1,0) and (1,2) ----
    \draw[black!55, dashed, line width=0.7pt]
      (axis cs:1,0,1) -- (axis cs:1,0,0)
      (axis cs:1,2,0.3333333) -- (axis cs:1,2,0);
    \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.3pt, color=black!70,
        forget plot] coordinates {(1,0,1) (1,2,0.3333333)};
    % ---- labels ----
    \node[font=\large, anchor=north west] at (axis description cs:0.0,0.98)
      {$z = \dfrac{x}{1+xy}$};
    \node[oivermillion, anchor=north, font=\small]
      at (axis cs:0.75,1.3,-0.12) {$R = [0,1]\times[0,2]$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-double-integral-questions">
<title>Concept Check</title>

<p>
The questions below are about <em>setting up</em> double integrals, not
about evaluating them. Throughout, <m>R</m> is the square
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-concept-check-square">
R = \left\{(x,y) \in \R^2 : -\tfrac12 \le x \le \tfrac12,\
-\tfrac12 \le y \le \tfrac12\right\} .
</men>

<p>
Each figure can be rotated: drag the background to turn it, and scroll to
zoom. In the first three figures the light blue segments at the corners of
<m>R</m> join the region of integration to the surface above it, the way
the dashed drops do in <xref ref="fig-volume-solid-geogebra"/>.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="q-di-sphere-over-square">
<title>A volume under a sphere</title>
<statement>
<p>
Write down a double integral for computing the volume inside the sphere
<m>x^2+y^2+z^2=1</m> and above the region <m>R</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-ws-sphere-over-square"/>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,5);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,4.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the double integral giving the volume under the sphere.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-ws-sphere-over-square">
<caption>
Figure (1): the part of the sphere <m>x^2+y^2+z^2=1</m> lying above the
square <m>R</m>. The four light blue segments join the corners of
<m>R</m> to the sphere above them.
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="ugutsgps"
             width="100%" aspect="500:500" design-width="500"
             material-width="500" material-height="500"
             shift-drag-zoom="yes" reset-icon="yes">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the picture and scroll to zoom. The shaded
square in the <m>xy</m>-plane is the region of integration <m>R</m>; the
mesh above it is the piece of the sphere whose volume the integral adds
up.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<exercise xml:id="q-di-signs">
<title>Signs, without computing</title>
<statement>
<p>
Assume that <m>R</m> is the square of
<xref ref="eq-concept-check-square"/> and choose all the correct
statements below. Remember to justify your answers. Note that
<xref ref="fig-ws-plane-x"/> and <xref ref="fig-ws-plane-y-minus-x"/>
are helpful in answering this question.
</p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>\displaystyle\iint_R x\,dA \gt 0</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>\displaystyle\iint_R x\,dA = 0</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>\displaystyle\iint_R x\,dA \lt 0</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>\displaystyle\iint_R (y-x)\,dA = 0</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>\displaystyle\iint_R (y-x)\,dA \lt 0</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>\displaystyle\iint_R (y-x)\,dA \gt 0</m></p></li>
</ol>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,7.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for choosing the correct statements and justifying them.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-ws-plane-x">
<caption>
Figure (2): the plane <m>z=x</m> over the square <m>R</m>, together with
<m>R</m> itself in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
</caption>
<!-- a local copy of the published material bske5ega with the corner
     drops added, in the style of the drops of figure (1) -->
<interactive platform="geogebra" width="100%" aspect="500:500">
<slate surface="geogebra" source="geogebra/dbl-fig2-plane-x.ggb"
       aspect="500:500" material-width="500" material-height="500"/>
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the picture and scroll to zoom.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-ws-plane-y-minus-x">
<caption>
Figure (3): the plane <m>z=y-x</m> over the square <m>R</m>. It meets the
<m>xy</m>-plane along the diagonal <m>y=x</m> of <m>R</m>.
</caption>
<!-- a local copy of the published material bysjzehd with corner drops;
     the other two corners of R lie on the plane, so they need none -->
<interactive platform="geogebra" width="100%" aspect="500:500">
<slate surface="geogebra" source="geogebra/dbl-fig3-plane-y-minus-x.ggb"
       aspect="500:500" material-width="500" material-height="500"/>
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the picture and scroll to zoom. The two
light blue segments rise to the corners <m>(-\tfrac12,\tfrac12)</m> and
<m>(\tfrac12,-\tfrac12)</m>, where <m>z=y-x</m> is <m>1</m> and
<m>-1</m>; at the other two corners the plane meets the
<m>xy</m>-plane.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

